    "md5": "fb2d1c9f199415092effc91186a7bb58",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1953,
    "chunk_text": "Cyclone Ditwah exposes growing climate threat to Sri Lanka\u2019s endemic flora\n\nCyclonic Storm Ditwah has once again underscored the increasing vulnerability of Sri Lanka\u2019s unique biodiversity to climate-driven extreme weather events, with scientists warning that many endemic flowering plants\u2014already confined to narrow ecological niches\u2014are facing heightened risks from floods, landslides and habitat disturbance.\n\nSri Lanka is globally recognised for its rich plant endemism. According to the National Red List (2020), the island is home to 3,087 indigenous angiosperm species belonging to 186 plant families, of which 863 species are endemic. These plants, ranging from towering forest trees to delicate herbs, climbers and epiphytes, are often restricted to specific climatic, altitudinal and microhabitat conditions, making them particularly sensitive to sudden environmental shocks.\n\nSenior Professor Siril Wijesundara, Research Professor at the National Institute of Fundamental Studies (NIFS), told The Island that cyclonic events, such as Ditwah, represent \u201ca serious and growing challenge to the long-term survival of Sri Lanka\u2019s endemic flora.\u201d\n\n\u201cMany of our endemic flowering plants occur in very limited geographical areas and within narrow ecological tolerances,\u201d Prof. Wijesundara said. \u201cWhen extreme rainfall, landslides or prolonged flooding affect these habitats, the impact can be irreversible, leading to local or even total extinction of species that exist nowhere else in the world.\u201d\n\nResearch carried out in 2016 by Sri Lankan scientists, using Geographic Information System (GIS) techniques, highlights the gravity of the situation. By analysing more than 84,000 plant location records across 2,847 grid cells of 5 km \u00d7 5 km, researchers identified nine major endemic areas: the Central Highlands, South Western Wet Zone, Northern Highlands, Eastern Highlands, Ritigala, Dolu Kanda, Yala, Wilpattu and Jaffna."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-01-04-islandlk-2108c373",
