    "chunk_size_bytes": 975,
    "chunk_text": "Experts stress that integrating biodiversity data with disaster risk information is now essential. Overlaying maps of endemic flowering plant distributions with GIS-based data on rainfall intensity, flood extent and landslide susceptibility can help identify areas where biological richness coincides with high disaster exposure.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s endemic flowering plants are an irreplaceable natural heritage. Cyclone Ditwah has highlighted how closely climate disasters and biodiversity loss are now intertwined. As extreme weather events become more frequent, conservationists argue that science-based, spatially informed planning is vital to safeguard the country\u2019s unique flora for future generations.\n\nSri Lanka after the 2025 Deluge and the NPP\u2019s Tidal Opportunity for 2026\n\nLeave a Reply Cancel replyYour email address will not be published. Required fields are marked *Comment * Name *\n\nSave my name, email, and website in this browser for the next time I comment."
